The eight women in the cabinet are: Yuriko Katsumata (Defence), Tomoko Aoki (Chief Cabinet Secretary), Seiran Mochizuki (Transport), Naoko Saito (Internal Affairs), Yuka Hasegawa (Education), Yumi Yamada (Environment), Shimane Yoshida (Gender Equality), and Shizuka Ono (Digital Sovereignty).

==Key policy commitments==

===Day One executive orders===
Within hours of being sworn in, the Ikazaki Cabinet issued three executive orders:

====The Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission====
{{main|Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission}}
Established by executive order on 20 May 2026, the {{Nihongo|'''Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission'''|山本日奈安全委員会|Yamamoto Hina Anzen Iinkai}} is an independent rail safety body named after the 17-year-old student killed in the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]]. The Commission has the unprecedented authority to ground any rail service in Okaiken without ministerial approval if it determines that staffing levels or safety protocols are insufficient. Transport Minister Seiran Mochizuki, the former OTWU organiser whose election victory was the largest margin of any OSP challenger, was given direct oversight of the Commission's implementation.

====The Kizan prosecution====
{{main|Kizan prosecution}}
Justice Minister Hiroshi Nakajima was tasked with pursuing the criminal prosecution of [[Kizan Heavy Industries]], the private contractor whose maintenance failures were identified as the proximate cause of the Nakayama Station incident. The prosecution—formally initiated on 22 May 2026—marked the first criminal case against a private infrastructure contractor in Sanese history. Nakajima, a former prosecutor with 14 years of experience at the [[Tokuyama District Public Prosecutors Office]] before entering politics, was selected specifically for his expertise in corporate criminal liability.

====The Two-Man Mandate====
The {{Nihongo|'''Two-Man Mandate'''|二人乗務義務|Futari Nōmu Gimu}}—a core OSP demand during the Three-Day War negotiations—requires all conventional rail services in Okaiken to operate with a minimum of two crew members. The mandate reversed the Honda cabinet's one-man operation policy that had been identified as a contributing factor in the Nakayama Station incident. Implementation oversight was assigned to Deputy Prime Minister Nakayama in his capacity as Minister of Social Affairs, with Transport Minister Mochizuki responsible for operational enforcement.

===Coalition legislation===

====The Digital Sovereignty Act====
{{main|Digital Sovereignty Act (Okaiken)}}
The SDP's flagship legislation, the {{Nihongo|'''Digital Sovereignty Act'''|デジタル主権法|Dejitaru Shuken Hō}}, mandates that all critical national infrastructure must maintain analogue backup systems and that no single AI system may control more than 40% of any essential service grid. The Act was a direct response to the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]], which had demonstrated the catastrophic consequences of centralized digital dependency. Minister for Digital Sovereignty Shizuka Ono was appointed to oversee implementation, with the Digital Agency restructured from its previous role as a modernisation body into a regulatory and resilience authority.

====The Yoshida Threshold====
The {{Nihongo|'''Yoshida Threshold'''|吉田閾値|Yoshida Shiki-ichi}}—the binding coalition condition extracted by SDP leader Shimane Yoshida—requires that at least 40% of all cabinet positions be held by women. The current cabinet exceeds this threshold at 47.1% (8 of 17 ministers). The Threshold is codified in the coalition agreement as a formal condition of SDP participation, meaning that any cabinet reshuffle that falls below 40% women would constitute a breach of the coalition and could trigger the SDP's withdrawal from government.

===External support===
Independent member [[Hayashi Akihiro]] (Ogasawara-3rd), elected on a platform of remote community resilience following his role in organising the civilian response during the Satori Blackout, announced his support for the coalition government. His statement—"The islands were forgotten once. The Digital Sovereignty Act ensures it cannot happen again."—directly linked his support to the SDP's digital resilience legislation, giving the coalition an effective working majority of 252 seats.

==Notable features==

The Ikazaki Cabinet is distinguished by several institutional firsts in Sanese political history:

* '''First formal gender threshold''': The Yoshida Threshold is the first binding gender composition requirement in any Sanese cabinet, and the first such threshold in any coalition agreement in Okaiken's modern democratic era.
* '''First criminal prosecution of a private contractor''': The Kizan prosecution is the first criminal case brought against a private infrastructure contractor for failures that resulted in loss of life.
* '''First independent rail safety commission with grounding authority''': The Yamamoto Hina Safety Commission's power to ground services without ministerial approval is unprecedented in Sanese transport governance.
* '''First Minister for Digital Sovereignty''': The creation of a dedicated Digital Sovereignty portfolio—separate from the previous Minister for Digital Transformation—reflects the paradigm shift from digital modernisation to digital resilience following the Satori Blackout.
* '''First Minister for Gender Equality and Democratic Accountability''': The combined portfolio, held by SDP leader Shimane Yoshida, links gender equality to democratic institutional reform, reflecting the SDP's position that democratic accountability is a gendered issue.
* '''First Deputy Prime Minister with a super-ministry''': Nakayama's dual role as Deputy PM and Minister of Social Affairs gives him coordinating oversight over welfare, health, and transport policy—a "super-ministry" structure without precedent in Sanese cabinet governance.

